  "final_answer": "DEPARTMENT OF POLICE\nGENERAL MANUAL PROCEDURE: UAS-2025-01\n\nTITLE: UNMANNED AIRCRAFT SYSTEMS (UAS) OPERATIONS\nEFFECTIVE DATE: JANUARY 15, 2025\nRESPONSIBLE OFFICE: TACTICAL SUPPORT DIVISION\nRELATED PROCEDURES: GM-400 (Use of Force), GM-500 (Vehicle Pursuits), FAA Part 107 Regulations\n\n1. PURPOSE\nThis policy establishes the guidelines for the deployment and operation of Unmanned Aircraft Systems (UAS) within the Department. The objective is to enhance officer and community safety, provide rapid aerial situational awareness, and serve as a tactical supplement to traditional aviation assets.\n\n2. DEFINITIONS\n- Unmanned Aircraft System (UAS): An aircraft operated without the possibility of direct human intervention from within or on the aircraft, including the ground control station and communication links.\n- Remote Pilot in Command (RPIC): A Department-certified officer responsible for the safe operation of the UAS.\n- Visual Observer (VO): A crew member who assists the RPIC in maintaining visual line of sight (VLOS) and situational awareness.\n\n3. POLICY\nDepartment personnel shall operate UAS in accordance with FAA Part 107 regulations and this policy. UAS deployment is authorized to support public safety missions where aerial perspective provides a tactical advantage.\n\n4. AUTHORIZED USE CASES\n- High-Risk Emergency Deployment: UAS may be deployed to provide real-time intelligence in calls involving firearms, barricaded suspects, or active threats, serving as a primary or supplemental aerial asset.\n- Rapid Response: UAS may be pre-staged or deployed to provide immediate aerial visuals prior to the arrival of ground units to assess scene safety.\n- Vehicle Pursuit Support: UAS may be utilized to track fleeing vehicles, providing continuous visual tracking to reduce the need for high-speed ground pursuits.\n- Tactical Team Integration: UAS shall be integrated into SWAT and tactical operations to provide reconnaissance, floor-plan mapping, and suspect location tracking.\n\n5. PROHIBITED USES\n- Surveillance of individuals or groups based solely on protected characteristics (race, religion, political affiliation).\n- Use of UAS for personal or non-departmental business.\n- Operation in violation of FAA Temporary Flight Restrictions (TFRs) without specific authorization.\n\n6. OPERATIONAL GUIDANCE\n- Data Synchronization: All flight logs and video metadata must be uploaded to the Department’s secure cloud repository within 24 hours of mission completion.\n- Scalability Strategy: The Department shall maintain a tiered deployment model: (1) Patrol-level micro-drones for immediate response; (2) Tactical-level heavy-lift drones for specialized operations.\n- Access Control/Security Scoping: Access to UAS video feeds is restricted to authorized personnel via encrypted VPN tunnels. All data access is logged with a unique user_id and timestamp.\n\n7. TRAINING\nAll operators must complete the Department’s UAS Certification Course, maintain a valid FAA Remote Pilot Certificate, and complete quarterly proficiency flight hours.\n\n8. KEY PERFORMANCE INDICATORS (KPIs)\n- Response Time Reduction: Average time from dispatch to aerial visual acquisition (Target: < 5 minutes).\n- Pursuit Success Rate: Percentage of pursuits resolved via UAS tracking without collision (Target: > 85%).\n- Incident De-escalation: Number of high-risk calls resolved without use of force due to UAS intelligence.\n\n9. RISK/MITIGATION\n| Risk Factor | Mitigation Strategy |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| Loss of Link | Automated 'Return to Home' (RTH) protocols and redundant signal frequencies. |\n| Privacy Concerns | Strict adherence to Fourth Amendment standards; no recording of private residences without warrant. |\n| Mid-Air Collision | Mandatory use of ADS-B In/Out and dedicated Visual Observers. |\n\n10. REFERENCES\n- Federal Aviation Administration. (2024). Part 107 Small Unmanned Aircraft Systems. https://www.faa.gov/uas/commercial_operators/part_107_operations\n- International Association of Chiefs of Police. (2023). UAS Policy Framework for Law Enforcement."
